WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.580
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:11.059
Scientists at the Seoul National University
in South Korea wrote a great paper on teaching

00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:18.079
an imaginary dragon all kinds of really cool
aerobatic maneuvers, like sharp turning, rapid

00:00:18.079 --> 00:00:21.769
winding, rolling, soaring, and diving.

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:26.390
This is all done by a reinforcement learning
variant, where the problem formulation is

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:32.189
that the AI has to continuously choose the
character’s actions to maximize a reward.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:37.690
Here, this reward function is related to a
trajectory which we can draw in advance, these

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.580
are the lines that the dragon seems follow
quite well.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.149
However, what you see here is the finished
product.

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:49.399
Curious to see how the dragon falters as it
learns to maneuver properly?

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:51.380
Well, we are in luck.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:52.380
Buckle up.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:57.860
You see the ideal trajectory here with black,
and initially, the dragon was too clumsy to

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.980
navigate in a way that even resembles this
path.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:07.570
Then, later, it learned to start the first
turn properly, but as you see here, it was

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:12.530
unable to avoid the obstacle and likely needs
to fly to the emergency room.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.750
But it would probably miss that building too,
of course.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.829
After more learning, it was able to finish
the first loop, but was still too inaccurate

00:01:20.829 --> 00:01:24.290
to perform the second.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:31.409
And finally, at last, it became adept at performing
this difficult maneuver.

00:01:31.409 --> 00:01:34.500
Applause!

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.640
One of the main difficulties of this problem
is the fact that the dragon is always in motion

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.840
and has a lot of momentum, and anything we
do always has an effect later, and we not

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.800
only have to find one good action, but whole
sequences of actions that will lead us to

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.930
victory.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.330
This is quite difficult.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.439
So how do we do that?

00:01:54.439 --> 00:01:59.380
To accomplish this, this work not only uses
a reinforcement learning variant, but also

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.689
adds something called self-regulated learning
to it, where we don’t present the AI with

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:09.610
a fixed curriculum, but we put the learner
in charge of its own learning.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:15.910
This also means that it is able to take a
big, complex goal and subdivide it into new,

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.530
smaller goals.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:22.580
In this case, the big goal is following the
trajectory with some more additional constraints,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:28.190
which, by itself, turned out to be too difficult
to learn with these traditional techniques.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:34.411
Instead, the agent realizes, that if it tracks
its own progress on a set of separate, but

00:02:34.411 --> 00:02:40.560
smaller subgoals, such as tracking its own
orientation, positions, and rotations against

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:47.510
the desired target states separately, it can
finally learn to perform these amazing stunts.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.840
That sounds great, but how is this done exactly?

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:56.160
This is done through a series of three steps,
where step one is generation, where the learner

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.900
creates a few alternative solutions for itself
and proceeds to the second step, evaluation,

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:07.560
where it has to judge these individual alternatives
and find the best ones.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:13.230
And third, learning, which means looking back
and recording whether these judgments indeed

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.660
put the learner in a better position.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.620
By iterating these three steps, this virtual
dragon learned to fly properly.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.040
Isn’t this amazing?

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:27.600
I mentioned earlier that this kind of problem
formulation is intractable without self-regulated

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.420
learning, and you can see here how a previous
work fares on following these trajectories.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:36.790
There is indeed a world of a difference between
the two.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:42.040
So there you go, in case you enter a virtual
world where you need to train your own dragon,

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.760
you’ll know what to do.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.310
But just in case, also read the paper in the
video description!

